  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2330b6f59de4c2349ad2089aeb64866f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::UIPromptController
  pages:
  - pageName: Page 1
    pageText: This experiment is to find the focal length of a convex lens by plotting
      graphs between u and v or between 1/u and 1/v. Identify the given components.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 2
    pageText: Start the experiment to find the focal length of the lens by the distant
      object method by dragging the lens holder onto the table and placing the lens
      on it so that it faces a distant object.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 3
    pageText: Now, place the screen on the other side of the lens on the table. To
      get a well-illuminated distinct image, make sure that sufficient light enters
      through the window and nothing obstructs its path.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 4
    pageText: Click and drag the screen towards and away from the lens and notice
      how the image changes.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 5
    pageText: Place the screen at a suitable position to obtain a clear, sharp, and
      inverted image of the tree.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 6
    pageText: Now measure the distance between the lens and the screen. Note it as
      the approximate focal length value.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 7
    pageText: Drag the apparatus and place it on the table.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 8
    pageText: Click on the optical bench to mark the lens position as O. Also, mark
      the points as F.,F2.2F,2F, on both sides of the lens,
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 9
    pageText: Drag the object needle and fix it in the upright at a point near 2F2
      on one side of the lens.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 10
    pageText: Enter the observed lens position, object position and object distance
      values in the observation table.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 11
    pageText: Use the given slider to adjust the height of the object needle to make
      its tip lie on the principal axis.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 12
    pageText: Observe through the other side of the lens. An inverted image of the
      object can be seen.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 13
    pageText: Now drag the image needle and place it near 2F1 on the other side of
      the lens.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 14
    pageText: Now move your eye towards the right and left of the pin using the slider.
      The tip of the needle gets separated, so there is a parallax error.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 15
    pageText: You can remove the parallax by dragging the image pin on the optical
      bench slowly.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 16
    pageText: Now, move your eye towards the right and left of the pin using the
      provided slider.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 17
    pageText: 'Enter the position of the image needle as observed on the optical
      bench. '
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 18
    pageText: Enter the value of the observed image distance by finding the difference
      between the position of the lens and the image needle.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 19
    pageText: We need to correct the index error. But our optical bench is well lubricated
      and calibrated such that the index error is 0.
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 1
    alternatePanels:
    - panel: {fileID: 314840037}
      stayInUpcomingPages: 0
      enableOnce: 0
      hasBeenEnabledOnce: 0
  - pageName: Page 20
    pageText: 'So, the corrected values of u and v remain unchanged and are

      tabulated.'
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 21
    pageText: 'Enter the correct values of 1/u, 1/v and focal length after rounding
      off to three decimal places.

      Note: Substitute the positive values of
      u and v to calculate the focal length.'
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 22
    pageText: 'Drag the object needle and fix it in the upright at a point near 2F2

      on
      one side of the lens.'
    showDialogBox: 1
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 23
    pageText: 'Enter the observed object position and object distance values in

      the
      observation table.'
    showDialogBox: 0
    showAlternatePanels: 0
    alternatePanels: []
  - pageName: Page 24
